# Shared by EVERY project built out of this repository.
#
# Two are: `fencepost` (the Python API, deployed by CLI from this directory) and
# `fencepost-web` (the front end, deployed from git with web/ as its Root Directory).
# Vercel reads this file for both, so an entry that is harmless to one can be fatal to the
# other. That is not hypothetical — see the note at the bottom.
#
# So the rule for this file is: only things that must never be uploaded by ANYBODY.
# Secrets and local artefacts. Nothing that is any project's source.
#
# Trimming the API's bundle does NOT belong here. It lives in vercel.json under
# functions["api/index.py"].excludeFiles, which is scoped to that one function and cannot
# reach another project's build.

.env*
*.db

# Plaintext redeemable share tokens. Gitignored as well, which is why they were easy to miss
# here: .gitignore keeps them out of the repository, this file keeps them out of deployments.
joincode*.txt
demo-secrets.txt

# --- Why this file is now this short -----------------------------------------------------
#
# On 2026-09-17 it gained `web/`, on the reasoning that the Python function has never needed
# a byte of the front end. The reasoning was right and the conclusion was wrong: fencepost-web
# builds from this same repository with web/ as its Root Directory, so the entry did not trim
# the API bundle — it deleted the front end's source.
#
# Nothing failed. Next.js found no app directory, emitted a single 404 page through the pages
# router, and Vercel deployed it and reported Ready:
#
#     Route (pages)          Size   First Load JS
#     ─ ○ /404               180 B         108 kB
#
# Every route on fencepost-app.vercel.app answered 404 for about an hour while the deployment
# status said everything was fine. scripts/check_deploy.py now catches exactly this.
